WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:01.824
- [Announcer] TGI Friday's,

00:00:01.824 --> 00:00:04.120
the restaurant synonymous
with classic American staples

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:07.272
like mozzarella sticks
and loaded potato skins.

00:00:07.272 --> 00:00:09.369
A dining oasis in suburbia.

00:00:09.369 --> 00:00:10.944
But what if the original concept of

00:00:10.944 --> 00:00:13.957
TGI Friday's had nothing to do with that?

00:00:13.957 --> 00:00:16.060
Enter Alan Stillman.

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:17.664
- My name is Alan Stillman,

00:00:17.664 --> 00:00:20.581
and I founded TGI Friday's in 1965.

00:00:24.716 --> 00:00:26.364
- [Announcer] TGI Friday's
started not as a restaurant,

00:00:26.364 --> 00:00:27.970
but a place to mingle.

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:29.396
- [Alan] The concept came about from

00:00:29.396 --> 00:00:33.384
being about 28 years old and
looking for a place to meet

00:00:33.384 --> 00:00:36.116
as many good-looking women as I could.

00:00:36.116 --> 00:00:39.041
- [Announcer] It was New York
City and the year was 1965.

00:00:39.041 --> 00:00:41.172
TGI Friday's came on
to the scene as one of

00:00:41.172 --> 00:00:43.884
the world's first singles bars,

00:00:43.884 --> 00:00:46.720
because back then, it wasn't
easy for singles to meet.

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:50.050
- [Alan] The technique at the
time was that during the week,

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:51.940
people would make phone calls.

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:54.806
They would organize with a group of people

00:00:54.806 --> 00:00:57.592
and you would all end up at
different cocktail parties,

00:00:57.592 --> 00:01:01.902
which basically is what TGI
Friday's was meant to be,

00:01:01.902 --> 00:01:04.611
a cocktail party that you
didn't need an invitation to.

00:01:04.611 --> 00:01:06.714
- [Announcer] Whether it
was a sound plan or not,

00:01:06.714 --> 00:01:09.746
TGI Friday's blew up almost instantly,

00:01:09.746 --> 00:01:11.830
attracting more singles
from the neighborhood

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:13.970
than anyone could have ever imagined.

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:16.510
- [Alan] I was there
one evening tending bar

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:19.902
and one of the waiters
came up to me and said,

00:01:19.902 --> 00:01:22.046
"Listen, there's half
a dozen people outside

00:01:22.046 --> 00:01:24.042
that want to come in, what do we do?"

00:01:24.042 --> 00:01:26.442
I walked out from behind the
bar about a half an hour later,

00:01:26.442 --> 00:01:29.334
and there were 20 people
standing out there,

00:01:29.334 --> 00:01:32.934
so we may have created the first line in

00:01:32.934 --> 00:01:34.971
the history of the restaurant business.

00:01:34.971 --> 00:01:35.934
- [Announcer] Alan came into this with

00:01:35.934 --> 00:01:38.020
no hospitality experience at all.

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:39.836
- [Alan] I would say less than none.

00:01:39.836 --> 00:01:44.003
I had a very inventive job, I
was selling perfumes at Flavors.

00:01:45.552 --> 00:01:47.182
- [Announcer] Alan became
so popular that Tom Cruise's

00:01:47.182 --> 00:01:50.991
character from the movie Cocktail
was loosely based on him.

00:01:50.991 --> 00:01:52.194
- [Alan] His character
sort of was imitating

00:01:52.194 --> 00:01:55.361
what we had done at Friday's at the time.

00:01:57.052 --> 00:01:58.766
He was trained with flipping bottles

00:01:58.766 --> 00:02:02.034
and drinks much better than I was.

00:02:02.034 --> 00:02:02.922
- [Announcer] And when he looks back,

00:02:02.922 --> 00:02:04.704
Alan's proud of what he created.

00:02:04.704 --> 00:02:07.011
- [Alan] It was certainly
as fun as anything

00:02:07.011 --> 00:02:09.550
that you could possibly imagine.

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:13.467
It was really something
that was quite special.

